// topic 11 – SQL & Queries  (7E 8M 5H)
q(3,11,"Which SQL clause filters rows before any grouping takes place?",["WHERE","HAVING","GROUP BY","ORDER BY"],0,"WHERE filters individual rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.","easy");
q(3,11,"Which statement retrieves all columns from table 'employees'?",["SELECT ALL FROM employees","SELECT * FROM employees","GET * FROM employees","FETCH * FROM employees"],1,"SELECT * FROM tablename retrieves every column from the specified table.","easy");
q(3,11,"What does the SQL keyword DISTINCT do?",["Sorts the result ascending","Removes duplicate rows from the result set","Filters rows on a condition","Groups rows with the same value"],1,"SELECT DISTINCT eliminates duplicate tuples from the query result.","easy");
q(3,11,"Which aggregate function counts ALL rows including NULLs?",["COUNT(column)","SUM(column)","COUNT(*)","AVG(column)"],2,"COUNT(*) counts every row in the result, regardless of NULL values.","easy");
q(3,11,"Which clause sorts a query result set?",["SORT BY","GROUP BY","HAVING","ORDER BY"],3,"ORDER BY arranges tuples in ascending (default) or descending order.","easy");
q(3,11,"What is the default sort direction of ORDER BY?",["Descending","Random","Ascending","Alphabetical only"],2,"Without DESC, ORDER BY sorts in ascending order by default.","easy");
q(3,11,"Which command removes all rows from a table but preserves the table structure?",["DROP TABLE","DELETE","TRUNCATE","ALTER"],2,"TRUNCATE deallocates data pages quickly while keeping the table schema intact.","easy");

q(3,11,"Which join type returns all rows from the left table and NULL for non-matching right-table rows?",["INNER JOIN","RIGHT OUTER JOIN","LEFT OUTER JOIN","CROSS JOIN"],2,"LEFT OUTER JOIN keeps every left-table row and fills NULLs where no right-table match exists.","medium");
q(3,11,"A subquery that returns exactly one row and one column is called a:",["Correlated subquery","Scalar subquery","Derived table","Inline view"],1,"A scalar subquery returns a single value usable anywhere a single-value expression is valid.","medium");
q(3,11,"Which clause filters groups produced by GROUP BY based on an aggregate condition?",["WHERE","HAVING","FILTER","ON"],1,"HAVING is evaluated after grouping; WHERE is evaluated before grouping.","medium");
q(3,11,"A CROSS JOIN between a 4-row table and a 5-row table produces how many rows?",["9","1","20","0"],2,"CROSS JOIN produces the Cartesian product: 4 × 5 = 20 rows.","medium");
q(3,11,"Which set operator returns only rows common to both SELECT statements?",["UNION","INTERSECT","EXCEPT","MINUS"],1,"INTERSECT returns the intersection of two result sets.","medium");
q(3,11,"A correlated subquery differs from a simple subquery in that it:",["Executes only once","References an outer-query column and re-executes for each outer row","Always uses GROUP BY","Cannot use aggregate functions"],1,"A correlated subquery is evaluated once per row of the outer query because it references outer columns.","medium");
q(3,11,"Which function returns the count of distinct values in a column?",["COUNT(*)","COUNT(DISTINCT column)","DISTINCT(column)","SUM(DISTINCT column)"],1,"COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts unique non-NULL values in the specified column.","medium");
q(3,11,"What does COALESCE(v1, v2, v3) return?",["Maximum of its arguments","First non-NULL value among its arguments","Always converts NULL to 0","Concatenates its arguments"],1,"COALESCE returns the first non-NULL value in the list, or NULL if all are NULL.","medium");

q(3,11,"SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(salary) > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM emp). This returns:",["All departments","Departments whose average salary exceeds the company-wide average","Departments with the maximum salary","Departments with the fewest employees"],1,"The subquery computes the overall average; HAVING keeps groups whose departmental average exceeds it.","hard");
q(3,11,"Which window function assigns a unique sequential integer per partition with no gaps?",["RANK()","DENSE_RANK()","ROW_NUMBER()","NTILE()"],2,"ROW_NUMBER() assigns consecutive integers starting at 1 per partition with no ties or gaps.","hard");
q(3,11,"How do RANK() and DENSE_RANK() differ after tied values?",["RANK is faster","DENSE_RANK leaves no gaps; RANK skips numbers after ties","RANK works per partition; DENSE_RANK does not","No difference"],1,"Tied rows get the same rank; RANK then skips (1,1,3) while DENSE_RANK does not (1,1,2).","hard");
q(3,11,"Which SQL behavior lets a transaction see its own uncommitted changes while other concurrent transactions cannot?",["Phantom read","Dirty read","Read-your-writes guarantee","Non-repeatable read"],2,"Read-your-writes (session consistency) lets a transaction observe its own in-progress changes.","hard");
q(3,11,"In logical query-processing order, which clause is evaluated LAST?",["FROM","WHERE","SELECT","ORDER BY"],3,"Logical order: FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY.","hard");

// topic 12 – Normalization  (7E 8M 5H)
q(3,12,"A relation is in 1NF when:",["All attributes are atomic and there are no repeating groups","There are no transitive dependencies","Every non-key attribute is fully dependent on the primary key","Each determinant is a candidate key"],0,"1NF requires atomic (indivisible) attribute values and no repeating groups or nested tables.","easy");
q(3,12,"Functional dependency X → Y means:",["Y uniquely determines X","X uniquely determines Y","X and Y are always equal","X and Y are independent"],1,"X → Y: any two tuples with the same X value must also have the same Y value.","easy");
q(3,12,"A relation is in 2NF if it is in 1NF and:",["Has no transitive dependencies","Every non-prime attribute is fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key","Every determinant is a candidate key","Has no multivalued dependencies"],1,"2NF eliminates partial dependencies – no non-key attribute depends on only part of a composite primary key.","easy");
q(3,12,"A candidate key is:",["Any attribute in a relation","A minimal superkey that uniquely identifies each tuple","A foreign key referencing another table","A key that allows NULL values"],1,"A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies each row.","easy");
q(3,12,"3NF eliminates which type of dependency?",["Partial dependency","Transitive dependency of non-prime attributes through non-candidate keys","Multivalued dependency","Join dependency"],1,"3NF: no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on any candidate key through another non-prime attribute.","easy");
q(3,12,"A superkey is:",["The primary key only","Any set of attributes that uniquely identifies tuples, not necessarily minimal","A foreign key constraint","A key with NULL values"],1,"A superkey may contain redundant attributes unlike a minimal candidate key.","easy");
q(3,12,"BCNF was designed to address anomalies that 3NF fails to handle when there are:",["No functional dependencies","Overlapping candidate keys","Multivalued dependencies","Only one candidate key"],1,"BCNF: for every non-trivial FD X → Y, X must be a superkey – stronger than 3NF.","easy");

q(3,12,"R(A,B,C) has FDs A→B and B→C; A is the primary key. Which normal form does this violate?",["1NF","2NF (partial dependency)","3NF (transitive dependency)","BCNF"],2,"B→C causes C to be transitively dependent on A through B, violating 3NF when B is not a candidate key.","medium");
q(3,12,"R(A,B,C,D) with A→B, C→D, and composite key AC. Which normal form is violated?",["1NF","2NF","3NF","BCNF"],1,"B depends only on A and D only on C – both are partial dependencies on the composite key, violating 2NF.","medium");
q(3,12,"A lossless-join decomposition guarantees that:",["The natural join of sub-relations reproduces the original relation exactly","The number of attributes is reduced","All FDs are preserved","The decomposition has exactly two sub-relations"],0,"Lossless-join: joining the sub-relations yields the original relation with no spurious tuples.","medium");
q(3,12,"For binary decomposition {R1, R2} of R to be lossless, the common attributes must:",["Functionally determine all attributes of R1 or all of R2","Have no common attributes","Cover all attributes of R","Be empty"],0,"The lossless-join condition: R1 ∩ R2 → R1 or R1 ∩ R2 → R2 (common attrs form a superkey in at least one sub-relation).","medium");
q(3,12,"A relation where every non-trivial FD has a superkey as determinant is in:",["1NF only","BCNF","3NF but not BCNF","5NF"],1,"BCNF: every non-trivial X → Y requires X to be a superkey.","medium");
q(3,12,"4NF addresses:",["Partial functional dependencies","Transitive functional dependencies","Non-trivial multivalued dependencies where the determinant is not a superkey","Join dependencies"],2,"4NF: for every non-trivial MVD X →→ Y, X must be a superkey.","medium");
q(3,12,"Dependency preservation in decomposition ensures that:",["The original relation is recoverable by joining","Each FD can be checked in one decomposed relation without joining","No spurious tuples are generated","NULL values are eliminated"],1,"Dependency preservation: every original FD can be enforced locally on one sub-relation, avoiding expensive joins for constraint checking.","medium");
q(3,12,"Armstrong's axioms for functional dependencies are:",["Reflexivity, Augmentation, Transitivity","Reflexivity, Commutativity, Transitivity","Augmentation, Commutativity, Reflexivity","Transitivity, Associativity, Augmentation"],0,"Armstrong's three axioms are Reflexivity, Augmentation, and Transitivity – they are sound and complete for FD derivation.","medium");

q(3,12,"R(A,B,C) has FDs AB→C and C→A; candidate keys are AB and BC. Is R in BCNF?",["Yes – all determinants are superkeys","No – C→A and C is not a superkey","No – AB→C violates BCNF","Yes – all FDs are trivial"],1,"C→A exists and C alone is not a superkey of R (since BC is a key, not C). This violates BCNF.","hard");
q(3,12,"Can every BCNF decomposition also be dependency-preserving?",["Yes, always","No – BCNF decomposition is not guaranteed to preserve all FDs; 3NF must be used when both lossless and dependency-preserving is required","Only for binary decompositions","Only when there are no overlapping keys"],1,"When a relation cannot be decomposed into BCNF losslessly AND dependency-preservingly, 3NF is the compromise.","hard");
q(3,12,"5NF (PJ/NF) is concerned with:",["Functional dependencies","Multivalued dependencies","Join dependencies not implied by candidate keys","Inclusion dependencies"],2,"5NF: every join dependency in the relation is implied by its candidate keys.","hard");
q(3,12,"The closure of attribute set {A} under FDs {A→B, B→C, C→D} is:",["{ A }","{ A, B }","{ A, B, C }","{ A, B, C, D }"],3,"A→B gives {A,B}; B→C gives {A,B,C}; C→D gives {A,B,C,D}.","hard");
q(3,12,"A relation in 3NF but NOT in BCNF must have:",["No functional dependencies","Overlapping candidate keys with at least one FD whose determinant is not a superkey","Multivalued dependencies","An attribute violating atomicity"],1,"Overlapping candidate keys can allow a prime attribute to be determined by a non-superkey, satisfying 3NF but failing BCNF.","hard");

// topic 13 – Transactions  (7E 8M 5H)
q(3,13,"The Atomicity property of ACID guarantees that:",["Each transaction executes in isolation","A transaction is either fully committed or fully rolled back – no partial updates","Committed data persists after crashes","Transactions do not conflict"],1,"Atomicity: all operations in a transaction succeed together, or all are undone.","easy");
q(3,13,"Which ACID property ensures the database moves from one valid state to another?",["Atomicity","Consistency","Isolation","Durability"],1,"Consistency: integrity constraints hold before and after every transaction.","easy");
q(3,13,"Durability means:",["Transactions are isolated from each other","Committed changes persist permanently even if the system crashes","Transactions are atomic","Data is consistent after every transaction"],1,"Durability: committed changes survive crashes, achieved via write-ahead logging and stable storage.","easy");
q(3,13,"Which SQL command permanently saves transaction changes?",["ROLLBACK","SAVEPOINT","COMMIT","BEGIN"],2,"COMMIT ends a transaction and makes all its changes durable.","easy");
q(3,13,"Which SQL command undoes all changes made by the current transaction?",["COMMIT","SAVEPOINT","BEGIN","ROLLBACK"],3,"ROLLBACK reverses all changes since the transaction began.","easy");
q(3,13,"A dirty read occurs when:",["A transaction reads committed data from another transaction","A transaction reads uncommitted data from another transaction that may later roll back","A transaction reads the same row twice and gets different values","A transaction reads from a non-existent table"],1,"A dirty read reads data written by an uncommitted concurrent transaction.","easy");
q(3,13,"Which isolation level permits dirty reads?",["READ COMMITTED","REPEATABLE READ","SERIALIZABLE","READ UNCOMMITTED"],3,"READ UNCOMMITTED is the lowest isolation level and allows dirty reads.","easy");

q(3,13,"A non-repeatable read occurs when:",["A transaction reads uncommitted data","A transaction reads the same row twice and gets different values because another committed transaction modified it in between","New rows appear that were not visible in an earlier read","Data is permanently lost"],1,"A non-repeatable read: row values change between two reads within the same transaction.","medium");
q(3,13,"What is a phantom read?",["Reading uncommitted data","Reading a row that no longer exists","A later read returns new rows not visible earlier because another transaction inserted them in between","A transaction reading its own uncommitted changes"],2,"Phantom reads occur when new rows inserted by another committed transaction appear in a re-executed range query.","medium");
q(3,13,"Which isolation level prevents dirty and non-repeatable reads but still allows phantom reads?",["READ UNCOMMITTED","READ COMMITTED","REPEATABLE READ","SERIALIZABLE"],2,"REPEATABLE READ locks read rows, preventing changes to them, but does not prevent phantom inserts.","medium");
q(3,13,"Two-Phase Locking (2PL) ensures serializability by:",["Executing transactions one at a time","Requiring all locks to be acquired in a growing phase and released only in a shrinking phase – never acquiring new locks after any release","Using timestamps for ordering","Logging all operations before execution"],1,"2PL's growing/shrinking structure prevents cyclic dependencies that would violate serializability.","medium");
q(3,13,"A deadlock is:",["When a transaction reads stale data","A cycle of transactions each waiting for a resource held by another in the cycle","When a transaction takes too long to commit","A conflict between read and write operations"],1,"Deadlock: circular wait where each transaction holds a resource needed by the next.","medium");
q(3,13,"Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) guarantees that:",["Reads are always consistent","All locks are released after commit","Log records are written to stable storage before corresponding data pages are written to disk","Transactions execute in serial order"],2,"WAL ensures log durability before data changes, enabling crash recovery (REDO/UNDO).","medium");
q(3,13,"Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) differs from pessimistic locking because OCC:",["Acquires locks at transaction start","Validates for conflicts only at commit time, assuming conflicts are rare","Is used only for read-only transactions","Uses timestamps exclusively"],1,"OCC lets transactions execute without locks; conflicts are detected at commit time.","medium");
q(3,13,"In database recovery, UNDO:",["Re-applies committed transactions lost in a crash","Reverses the effects of uncommitted transactions active at crash time","Flushes dirty pages to disk","Creates a new checkpoint"],1,"UNDO rolls back loser (uncommitted) transactions to restore consistency after a crash.","medium");

q(3,13,"Which schedule type is guaranteed serializable?",["Any schedule using shared locks","Any schedule produced by Strict Two-Phase Locking (S2PL)","Any schedule where transactions run in parallel","Any schedule under READ COMMITTED isolation"],1,"Strict 2PL holds all exclusive locks until after commit/abort, guaranteeing conflict serializability and preventing cascading aborts.","hard");
q(3,13,"Conflict serializability vs. view serializability:",["Conflict serializability is stronger and implies view serializability; view serializability is broader","View serializability is stronger","They are equivalent","Conflict serializability applies only to reads"],0,"Every conflict-serializable schedule is view-serializable, but not vice versa.","hard");
q(3,13,"Timestamp-based concurrency control orders transactions by:",["Lock acquisition order","Transaction start timestamps; conflicting operations are allowed only if they follow the timestamp order, otherwise the younger transaction aborts","Primary key values","Transaction size"],1,"Each transaction receives a unique timestamp; operations must respect that order to maintain serializability.","hard");
q(3,13,"The ARIES recovery algorithm uses which mechanism to identify REDO and UNDO sets?",["The commit log only","An Analysis pass that builds a dirty-page table and transaction table, followed by REDO and UNDO passes","A checkpoint scan only","Buffer pool contents"],1,"ARIES three passes: Analysis (who is dirty/active), REDO (replay all changes), UNDO (roll back losers).","hard");
q(3,13,"A cascadeless schedule requires that:",["No transaction reads data written by an uncommitted transaction","All transactions commit before writing","All reads happen before any writes","Transactions execute serially"],0,"Cascadeless schedules prevent cascading aborts by reading only committed data.","hard");

// topic 14 – Indexing  (7E 8M 5H)
q(3,14,"What is the primary purpose of a database index?",["Enforce referential integrity","Speed up data retrieval by reducing disk accesses","Compress data on disk","Encrypt sensitive columns"],1,"An index allows the database engine to locate rows quickly without a full table scan.","easy");
q(3,14,"Which tree structure is most commonly used to implement database indexes?",["Binary search tree","AVL tree","B+ tree","Red-black tree"],2,"B+ trees store all data pointers in linked leaf nodes, supporting both point lookups and range scans efficiently.","easy");
q(3,14,"In a clustered index, rows are stored physically in:",["Random order","Insertion order","The same order as the index key","Descending order always"],2,"A clustered index dictates the physical row order; only one per table is possible.","easy");
q(3,14,"How many clustered indexes can a table have?",["Unlimited","One","Two","One per column"],1,"Physical storage can be ordered in only one way, so only one clustered index is allowed.","easy");
q(3,14,"A non-clustered index stores:",["Actual table rows in index order","Index key values and row-locator pointers to the actual data rows","Only NULL values","Only unique column values"],1,"A non-clustered index contains key values plus pointers (heap RIDs or clustered key values) to the data rows.","easy");
q(3,14,"Which index type is best for equality lookups but cannot support range queries?",["B+ tree index","Hash index","Bitmap index","Full-text index"],1,"Hash indexes compute a hash for O(1) equality lookup but have no ordering for range predicates.","easy");
q(3,14,"A covering index is one that:",["Spans the entire table","Contains all columns referenced by a query, enabling an index-only scan without accessing the table","Has no NULL values","Is a clustered index on one column"],1,"A covering index satisfies the query entirely from the index, avoiding table lookups.","easy");

q(3,14,"In a B+ tree of order m, an internal node holds at most how many keys?",["m","m - 1","m + 1","2m"],1,"Order m means at most m child pointers and therefore at most m-1 keys per internal node.","medium");
q(3,14,"The leftmost prefix rule for composite indexes means:",["All indexed columns must appear in the WHERE clause","The index on (A,B,C) benefits queries filtering on A, (A,B), or (A,B,C) but NOT on B or C alone","A composite index always covers all columns","The leftmost column must be the primary key"],1,"Only leading prefix subsets of a composite index can be used by the optimizer.","medium");
q(3,14,"Index selectivity is:",["The number of indexes on a table","The ratio of distinct values to total rows; high selectivity makes the index more useful","The physical size of the index on disk","The number of columns in a composite index"],1,"High selectivity (close to 1.0) means each key value identifies few rows, making the index effective.","medium");
q(3,14,"A bitmap index is most appropriate for:",["High-cardinality columns like email addresses","Low-cardinality columns in read-heavy analytical workloads","Columns that are frequently updated","Primary key columns"],1,"Bitmap indexes use one bit per row per distinct value, ideal for low-cardinality OLAP columns.","medium");
q(3,14,"A dense index has:",["One entry per disk block","One entry per record in the data file","Only unique keys","Entries only in memory"],1,"A dense index contains an entry for every search key value (and thus every record).","medium");
q(3,14,"A sparse index has:",["Entries for only some records (typically one per data block)","Entries for NULL values only","Entries stored in a separate file","An entry for every column in the table"],0,"A sparse index stores one entry per block (usually the anchor record); sequential scan finds the rest within each block.","medium");
q(3,14,"Which index type is used for full-text keyword search over large textual data?",["B+ tree index","Hash index","Inverted (full-text) index","Bitmap index"],2,"Inverted indexes map terms to the documents/rows containing them, enabling efficient keyword search.","medium");
q(3,14,"The main maintenance overhead of indexes on write-heavy tables is:",["Increased read time","Additional storage and the need to update every relevant index on each INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE","Reduced query plan options","Increased network bandwidth"],1,"Every write must update all applicable indexes, adding I/O and compute overhead.","medium");

q(3,14,"In a B+ tree of order 4 (max 4 pointers per node), the minimum number of keys in a non-root internal node is:",["1","2","3","4"],0,"Order 4: min children in non-root internal node = ceil(4/2) = 2, so min keys = 2-1 = 1.","hard");
q(3,14,"Why might the query optimizer choose a full table scan over an available index?",["Indexes are always slower","When a large fraction of rows is returned, sequential scan cost is lower than random I/O through the index","Indexes can only be used for equality predicates","The optimizer never uses indexes for large tables"],1,"When selectivity is low (many rows returned), random I/O via the index exceeds the cost of one sequential scan.","hard");
q(3,14,"An index-organized table (IOT / clustered table) is one where:",["The table has no indexes","The table rows are stored directly in the B+ tree leaf nodes of the primary key index, eliminating a separate heap","Only one index is allowed","The table is read-only and stored in memory"],1,"In an IOT, the B+ tree IS the table – rows live in the leaf pages, removing the extra heap storage layer.","hard");
q(3,14,"For a 1,000,000-row table with a B+ tree index of height 3, an equality lookup requires approximately how many disk I/Os?",["1,000,000","1,000","3 to 4","50"],2,"B+ tree traversal: 1 I/O per level (3 levels) + 1 I/O to fetch the data row ≈ 3–4 I/Os total.","hard");
q(3,14,"A low-selectivity index on a high-write OLTP table creates:",["Faster reads with no write overhead","Write amplification: each write must update the low-value index with little read benefit","Improved range scan performance","Reduced storage requirements"],1,"Low-selectivity indexes are often ignored by the optimizer for reads yet always maintained on writes – pure overhead.","hard");

// topic 15 – ER Model  (7E 8M 5H)
q(3,15,"In the ER model, an entity is:",["A relationship between two tables","A real-world object or concept that has a distinct existence and about which data is stored","A column in a relational table","A constraint on attribute values"],1,"An entity is a distinct, identifiable object or concept represented in the database (e.g., Student, Product).","easy");
q(3,15,"An attribute in the ER model is:",["A relationship between two entities","A property or characteristic that describes an entity","A table in the database","A foreign key constraint"],1,"Attributes describe entities (e.g., Student has StudentID, Name, Age).","easy");
q(3,15,"A key attribute is one whose values:",["Have a default value","Uniquely identify each entity in an entity set","Are multi-valued","Are derived from other attributes"],1,"Key attributes (underlined in ER diagrams) uniquely identify each entity instance.","easy");
q(3,15,"A weak entity is one that:",["Has no attributes","Cannot be uniquely identified by its own attributes alone and depends on an owner (strong) entity","Has only one attribute","Stores aggregate data"],1,"A weak entity uses a partial key combined with its owner entity's key to achieve unique identification.","easy");
q(3,15,"An M:N cardinality ratio means:",["Each entity on one side relates to at most one on the other","Entities on either side can be associated with many entities on the other side","One entity on the left maps to many on the right only","No entity can participate in this relationship"],1,"M:N: an instance of A can relate to multiple instances of B and vice versa.","easy");
q(3,15,"Total participation (double line in ER diagrams) means:",["Every entity instance must participate in at least one relationship instance","Entity instances may or may not participate","The relationship has maximum cardinality 1","The relationship is recursive"],0,"Total participation = existence dependency: every entity instance must be in the relationship.","easy");
q(3,15,"A derived attribute is:",["An attribute whose value is stored physically and cannot be computed","An attribute whose value can be computed from other stored attributes","A primary key attribute","A multivalued attribute"],1,"Derived attributes (dashed oval in ER diagrams) are computable from stored data (e.g., Age from BirthDate).","easy");

q(3,15,"When mapping a 1:N relationship to a relational schema, the standard approach is to:",["Create a separate join table","Place the primary key of the '1' side as a foreign key in the 'N' side relation","Merge both entities into one table","Place the foreign key of the 'N' side in the '1' side relation"],1,"For 1:N: the PK of the 'one' entity becomes a FK in the 'many' entity's table.","medium");
q(3,15,"When mapping an M:N relationship to a relational schema, you must:",["Add a foreign key to one of the entity tables","Create a junction relation containing the PKs of both entities as a composite key","Merge both entities into one table","Delete one entity type"],1,"An M:N relationship maps to a junction table with both entity PKs as foreign keys forming the composite PK.","medium");
q(3,15,"A recursive (unary) relationship is one where:",["An entity participates twice in the same relationship type, relating to instances of the same entity set","A relationship has more than two entity types","An entity has no attributes","A weak entity has multiple identifying relationships"],0,"A self-referencing relationship connects an entity to itself (e.g., Employee 'manages' Employee).","medium");
q(3,15,"A composite attribute is:",["An attribute that can take multiple values simultaneously","An attribute made up of multiple sub-attributes (e.g., Address = Street + City + Zip)","An attribute whose value is computed","An attribute that is part of a foreign key"],1,"Composite attributes are divisible into sub-parts with independent meanings.","medium");
q(3,15,"A ternary relationship involves:",["One entity type","Two entity types","Three entity types","More than three entity types only"],2,"A ternary relationship is a simultaneous association among exactly three entity types.","medium");
q(3,15,"The identifying relationship of a weak entity is:",["Any relationship it participates in","The specific relationship through which the weak entity derives its complete identification from its owner strong entity","A relationship with cardinality 1:1","A derived attribute relationship"],1,"The identifying relationship (double diamond in ER diagrams) connects the weak entity to its owner entity.","medium");
q(3,15,"Specialization vs. generalization in ER modeling:",["They are identical concepts","Specialization is top-down (superclass refined into subclasses); generalization is bottom-up (subclasses unified into a superclass)","Generalization is top-down; specialization is bottom-up","Specialization applies only to attributes"],1,"Specialization refines (top-down); generalization abstracts (bottom-up) – complementary design processes.","medium");
q(3,15,"When an ISA hierarchy uses the 'overlap' constraint, an entity:",["Can belong to at most one subclass","Can belong to more than one subclass simultaneously","Must belong to some subclass","Has no attributes from the superclass"],1,"Overlap constraint: an entity instance may be a member of multiple subclasses at the same time.","medium");

q(3,15,"For an M:N relationship with its own attribute, the attribute must be placed in:",["One of the entity tables","A separate junction table alongside the foreign keys of both entities","A view joining both entity tables","A derived attribute of either entity"],1,"M:N relationship attributes depend on the combination of both entity keys, so they belong in the junction table.","hard");
q(3,15,"Mapping a multivalued attribute when converting ER to relational schema requires:",["Repeating the column in the entity table","A separate relation with the entity's PK as FK and the multivalued attribute as part of a composite key","Storing it as a JSON blob","Removing it as it cannot be represented relationally"],1,"Multivalued attributes map to a separate relation whose composite PK is the entity PK plus the attribute value.","hard");
q(3,15,"Which ISA mapping strategy is best when queries frequently access all attributes of specific subtypes?",["Single table for the entire hierarchy","Table-per-subtype (joined to superclass table)","Table-per-concrete-class (one table per leaf subclass, duplicating superclass attributes)","No standard mapping exists"],2,"Table-per-concrete-class puts all attributes in each subtype table, avoiding joins for subtype-specific queries.","hard");
q(3,15,"Employee(EID,Name), Project(PID,Title), Works_On relationship with attribute Hours. After ER-to-relational mapping, Works_On becomes:",["An attribute in Employee","An attribute in Project","A table Works_On(EID, PID, Hours) with primary key (EID, PID)","A view joining Employee and Project"],2,"Works_On is M:N with an attribute, so it maps to a junction table with composite PK (EID, PID) and attribute Hours.","hard");
q(3,15,"Converting a ternary relationship R(A,B,C) into binary relationships risks:",["No problem – always equivalent","Losing semantic information because binary decomposition may not capture the same combination constraints as the ternary relationship","Doubling the number of tables","Duplicating all attributes"],1,"Decomposing a ternary into binaries can allow invalid combinations that the ternary constraint would have prevented.","hard");

